ABSTRACT = [
    'Simulating quantum algorithms and quantum many-body systems on classical hardware is limited by the exponential size of the state vector: a 50-qubit state needs about 18 petabytes. Tensor networks overcome this by writing the state as a network of small tensors whose cost is set by a bond dimension, and the best network depends on the entanglement structure of the problem. This project benchmarks three representative families on a CPU: matrix product states (MPS) and tensor trains (TT) for circuit simulation, projected entangled pair states (PEPS) for two-dimensional lattices, and the multiscale entanglement renormalization ansatz (MERA) for critical systems.',
    'Three studies were carried out, each measured against an exact reference. In Study 1 the Deutsch–Jozsa algorithm (10 to 30 qubits) and a controlled modular-exponentiation kernel of Shor’s algorithm (8 to 13 qubits) were simulated on MPS/TT with bond caps of 4 to 32. In Study 2 the ground state of the two-dimensional Heisenberg model on 2×2, 3×3 and 4×4 lattices was computed with PEPS by imaginary-time simple update and by a layered variational quantum eigensolver (VQE) whose gates are applied with truncation and whose truncated energy is optimized directly. In Study 3 a binary MERA was built and optimized for the critical transverse-field Ising chain, validated against a state-vector evaluation and finite differences.',
    'The Deutsch–Jozsa MPS stayed at bond rank 1 with zero error up to 30 qubits, and the Shor kernel reached machine precision (about 10^{-15}) once the bond cap equalled the true rank of 8 to 16. For PEPS, optimizing the VQE through the truncation reduced the error by a factor of 2 to 6, reaching 3.0×10^{-3} on 3×3 at D = 6 and 1.1×10^{-2} on 4×4 at D = 4 relative to exact diagonalization. The MERA reached a relative energy error of 1.0×10^{-3} at L = 16 and χ = 4, with the largest-χ run not yet converged.',
    'The work was implemented in Python (NumPy, SciPy, opt_einsum, quimb) and the results are collected in an interactive Streamlit dashboard. The limits are stated plainly: the PEPS study covers the Heisenberg model on lattices up to 4×4, the Shor kernel is a simplified surrogate, the MPS/TT memory measurement is unreliable, and the MERA benchmark grid over system size is not yet complete. Extensions to fermionic models, larger lattices and the full MERA campaign are identified as future work.',
]

